<project name="U5L1p7-RemDups" app="Snap! 11.0.8, https://snap.berkeley.edu" version="2"><notes></notes><thumbnail>data:image/png;base64,</thumbnail><scenes select="1"><scene name="U5L1p7-RemDups"><notes></notes><hidden></hidden><headers></headers><code></code><blocks><block-definition s="are the items of %&apos;data&apos; distinct?" type="predicate" category="lists"><header></header><code></code><translations></translations><inputs><input type="%l"></input></inputs><script><block s="doIf"><block s="reportListIsEmpty"><block var="data"/></block><script><block s="doReport"><block s="reportBoolean"><l><bool>true</bool></l></block></block></script><list></list></block><block s="doIf"><block s="reportListContainsItem"><block s="reportCDR"><block var="data"/></block><block s="reportListItem"><l>1</l><block var="data"/></block></block><script><block s="doReport"><block s="reportBoolean"><l><bool>false</bool></l></block></block></script><list></list></block><block s="doReport"><custom-block s="are the items of %l distinct?"><block s="reportCDR"><block var="data"/></block></custom-block></block></script></block-definition><block-definition s="distinct items from %&apos;data&apos;" type="reporter" category="lists"><header></header><code></code><translations></translations><inputs><input type="%l"></input></inputs><script><block s="doIf"><block s="reportListIsEmpty"><block var="data"/></block><script><block s="doReport"><block s="reportNewList"><list></list></block></block></script><list></list></block><block s="doIf"><block s="reportListContainsItem"><block s="reportCDR"><block var="data"/></block><block s="reportListItem"><l>1</l><block var="data"/></block></block><script><block s="doReport"><custom-block s="distinct items from %l"><block s="reportCDR"><block var="data"/></block></custom-block></block></script><list></list></block><block s="doReport"><block s="reportCONS"><block s="reportListItem"><l>1</l><block var="data"/></block><custom-block s="distinct items from %l"><block s="reportCDR"><block var="data"/></block></custom-block></block></block></script></block-definition></blocks><primitives></primitives><stage name="Stage" width="480" height="360" costume="0" color="255,255,255,1" tempo="60" threadsafe="false" penlog="false" volume="100" pan="0" lines="round" ternary="false" hyperops="true" codify="false" inheritance="true" sublistIDs="false" id="91"><pentrails>data:image/png;base64,</pentrails><costumes><list struct="atomic" id="92"></list></costumes><sounds><list struct="atomic" id="93"></list></sounds><variables></variables><blocks></blocks><scripts></scripts><sprites select="1"><sprite name="Sprite" idx="1" x="0" y="0" heading="90" scale="1" volume="100" pan="0" rotation="1" draggable="true" costume="0" color="80,80,80,1" pen="tip" id="98"><costumes><list struct="atomic" id="99"></list></costumes><sounds><list struct="atomic" id="100"></list></sounds><blocks></blocks><variables></variables><scripts><script x="13.333333333333343" y="13.333333333333329"><custom-block s="are the items of %l distinct?"><l/><comment w="54" collapsed="true">5.1.7.1</comment></custom-block></script><comment x="13.333333333333343" y="43.66666666666666" w="332" collapsed="false">Commentary on 5.1.7.1:  There are several things to learn in this problem. The one that probably jumps out is about recursion. and in particular about how a recursive call has to get closer to a base case, which in this case means closer to the input being empty -- or, in other words, being smaller than the input value in the current invocation of the procedure.  But the real study of recursive functions is in Unit 8; this is just a preview.&#xD;&#xD;A more important lesson at this point in the course is the idea of /case analysis/.  The code distinguishes three cases, each with a different algorithm.&#xD;&#xD;Case 1:  The input list is empty.  It may not be immediately obvious what the right answer is.  Are the items distinct if there aren&apos;t any items?  It&apos;s tempting to say no; if there are no items, there are no distinct items.  The right way to think about it, though, is that if there are no equal items, then the items are distinct.  Imagine writing another block called &quot;Are there any pairs of equal items in (list)?&quot;  The value reported by /that/ block should be the opposite of the value reported by this block, and for an empty list, that other block should clearly say no, there aren&apos;t any pairs of equal items.  So our block should report True.&#xD;&#xD;Case 1 is the base case of the recursive algorithm, but (even when learning about recursion) don&apos;t think of it that way!  The case of an empty list isn&apos;t just a weird, meaningless question that you need in a recursive program for some reason.  It&apos;s a perfectly valid question in its own right!  Are the items of an empty list distinct?  As we&apos;ve discussed above, there&apos;s a right answer to this question: True.  Whatever&apos;s the right answer for the question in itself will also be the right answer as the recursive base case.&#xD;&#xD;Case 2: The first item of the list is contained in the butfirst of the list (all but the first item).  In this case, are the items distinct?  This is the easy case: No, they&apos;re not all distinct, because the first item appears twice.  So the block should report False in this case.&#xD;&#xD;Case 3: The first item of the list doesn&apos;t appear in the rest of the list.  So is the answer True or False?  It&apos;s tempting to say True, because we know one &quot;distinct item.&quot;  But in fact we don&apos;t know the answer yet.  We have to look for equal items later in the list.  What we do know is that the first item of the list isn&apos;t going to be the reason to say False.  So we can drop that item from the list, and whatever the answer is for the resulting smaller list is also the answer for the complete list.&#xD;&#xD;There are all sorts of possible misunderstandings for case 3.  For example, in the recursive call, shouldn&apos;t the code look at item 2 of the list?  There are two good answers to that question.  The easiest answer is, never mind what happens in the recursive call.  Just assume that it will give you the right answer to the (different) question you&apos;re asking it.  For /your/ question, you look at item 1.  The longer answer is that inside the computer there are a bunch of little people, each of whom knows how to run a particular procedure.  In particular, there are several DISTINCT? little people.  So if I want to know whether the items of {a,b,c,d} are distinct, I hire Diana, who&apos;s a DISTINCT? specialist, and ask her to put {a,b,c,d} in her pocket.  She goes through the code of the block, and ends up hiring David, /another/ DISTINCT? specialist.  She gives him the list {b.c.d} to put in /his/ pocket.  Diana&apos;s pocket has one list; David&apos;s pocket has a different list.  David will answer the question he was asked, and reports True to Diana.  Diana&apos;s code says to report to me whatever David reports to her. </comment><comment x="13.333333333333343" y="777" w="331.3333333333333" collapsed="false">5.1.7.3:  The answer is that the time more than doubles -- in fact it is multiplied by four.  (The time is quadratic in the size of the list.)  This is the first time we&apos;re asking students this sort of question and expecting them to figure out the answer (rather than run timing tests).  So it&apos;s not a disaster if they don&apos;t succeed.  In order to analyze the running time of DISTINCT?, they have to know that the CONTAINS primitive predicate takes linear time, not constant time.  (Students should be able to understand why: the algorithm has to check every item of the list to see if it&apos;s equal to the desired value, like a linear search.  We told them that on page 3 of this lab.)  So, Diana&apos;s time is proportional to the size of the input list, plus however long David takes.  But David, too, has a CONTAINS block to run. So his time is proportional to the size of his input, plus however long Della, the little person he hires for his recursive call, takes.  By the time we get an answer, there&apos;s a little person for each item of the list, and each of them requires linear time, so that&apos;s n little people times n steps in CONTAINS = n^2 time.  (Yes, each little person has a different size list, so really you have to add up all the numbers from one to n, but that&apos;s still proportional to n^2.  (It&apos;s actually about n^2/2, but the exact amount isn&apos;t as important as the fact that it&apos;s quadratic.)&#xD;&#xD;If a pair of students actually carries out a timing experiment, good for them!  Don&apos;t yell at them.</comment><script x="13.333333333333343" y="1078.3333333333333"><custom-block s="distinct items from %l"><l/><comment w="53.333333333333336" collapsed="true">5.1.7.5</comment></custom-block></script><comment x="13.333333333333343" y="1108.6666666666667" w="326.6666666666667" collapsed="false">The predicate in 5.1.7.2 is practice for this problem.  The structure of this procedure is /exactly the same/ as that other one; students who are on the ball will start by duplicating the code of the predicate and dragging it into this block.  The questions asked by the two IF blocks are unchanged in this code.  The reported values are different, though.  So to complete this problem, start by ignoring the code and asking what the block should report in each case.  What if the input is empty?  It&apos;s much easier to figure out what to report here than it was for the predicate.  What if the first item appears in the butfirst of the list?  This is a little more complicated than the predicate.  What the block should return is exactly what a recursive call will return with the first item left out.&#xD;&#xD;But it&apos;s the third case that really stretches the mind.  This is where the IN FRONT OF block is important.  If the first item /isn&apos;t/ in the rest of the list, then we have to include that first item in the result; it&apos;s distinct from all other items of the list.  What else is in the result?  The items we get from a recursive call on the butfirst of the list.&#xD;&#xD;A picture might help:&#xD;&#xD;                                        (a list)&#xD;                                      /          \&#xD;                                    /             \&#xD;                                  /                 \&#xD;                     ITEM 1 OF           ALL BUT FIRST OF&#xD;                           |                                    |&#xD;                           |                                    |&#xD;                           |                                    |&#xD;                 (some computation)          (recursive call)&#xD;                                   \                           /&#xD;                                    \                         /&#xD;                                     \                      /&#xD;                                     IN FRONT OF&#xD;&#xD;Split a list into its first item and all the rest of the items.  Since its first item is just one value, you can do whatever computation on it is needed.  IN FRONT OF is sort of the inverse of ALL BUT FIRST OF.&#xD;&#xD;In this case, the &quot;some computation&quot; is nothing; it&apos;s the same ITEM 1 OF that gets prepended to the result of the recursive call. </comment></scripts></sprite></sprites></stage><variables></variables></scene></scenes></project>